{"id":"neet-2026r-q093","pn":93,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Animal Kingdom","st":"Reptilia characteristics","q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I : The class name Reptilia refers to creeping or crawling mode of locomotion.\nStatement II : All organisms belonging to Reptilia have three chambered heart.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"c":1,"s":"The name Reptilia refers to their creeping or crawling mode of locomotion.\nThe heart is usually three-chambered, but four-chambered in crocodiles.\nSo not all reptiles have a three-chambered heart."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q104","pn":104,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Animal Kingdom","st":"Characters of chordates","q":"Which of the following is not a characteristic of chordates?","o":["Central nervous system is dorsal","Absence of gills","Presence of post anal part (tail)","Presence of notochord"],"c":1,"s":"Members of phylum Chordata show these features at some stage of life:\nPresence of notochord.\nPharynx perforated by gill slits.\nDorsal, hollow and single central nervous system.\nVentral heart.\nA post-anal tail.\nGill slits are absent in non-chordates, so 'absence of gills' is not a chordate character.\nNote: Question sits in the Botany block of the booklet but is Zoology content; sub set to Zoology."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q133","pn":133,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Animal Kingdom","st":"Symmetry in animals","q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : When any plane passing through the central axis of the body divides the organism into two identical halves, it is called radial symmetry.\nStatement II : In phylum Echinodermata, both adults and larvae are radially symmetrical.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"c":1,"s":"Statement I is correct: a plane through the central axis giving two identical halves means radial symmetry.\nEchinoderms show radial or bilateral symmetry depending on the stage.\nAdults are radially symmetrical, but larvae are bilaterally symmetrical.\nSo Statement II is incorrect."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q142","pn":142,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Animal Kingdom","st":"Class Cyclostomata","q":"A group of researchers procured some fish like animals and upon investigation the following characters were observed:\nA. Endoskeleton was made of cartilage.\nB. Ectoparasitic; as they were found attached on fish skin with their circular sucking mouth.\nC. Paired fins and scales were absent, but 7 pairs of gill slits were present.\nWhich of the following species of animals did they consider to fit best with these characters?","o":["Exocoetus sp.","Branchiostoma sp.","Petromyzon sp.","Scoliodon sp"],"c":2,"s":"Petromyzon has a cartilaginous endoskeleton.\nIt has a circular sucking mouth and is an ectoparasite on some fishes.\nIts body has no scales and no paired fins.\nIt has 7 pairs (6-15 pairs) of gill slits.\nScoliodon and Exocoetus are not parasites."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q150","pn":150,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Animal Kingdom","st":"Aves: flightless birds","q":"The flightless bird with forelimbs modified as paddle-like structures suited for swimming is known as:","o":["Struthio","Psittacula","Neophron","Aptenodytes"],"c":3,"s":"Neophron is a vulture and Psittacula is a parrot. Both fly.\nStruthio is an ostrich and Aptenodytes is a penguin. Both are flightless.\nIn penguins the forelimbs are modified into flippers (paddle-like) used for swimming.\nIn ostriches the forelimbs are small and used for balance while running, not for swimming."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q153","pn":153,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Animal Kingdom","st":"Class Osteichthyes","q":"Select the set of fishes which belong to the class Osteichthyes:","o":["Devil fish, Cuttlefish and Hagfish","Starfish, Hagfish and Cuttlefish","Flying fish, Angel fish and Fighting fish","Saw fish, Fighting fish and Dog fish"],"c":2,"s":"Option 3 is correct.\nFlying fish (Exocoetus) is a marine bony fish. Angel fish (Pterophyllum) and fighting fish (Betta) are aquarium bony fishes.\nOption 1 is wrong: devil fish (Octopus) and cuttlefish (Sepia) are molluscs. Hag fish (Myxine) is a cyclostome.\nOption 4 is wrong: saw fish (Pristis) and dog fish (Scoliodon) are cartilaginous fishes.\nOption 2 is wrong: starfish (Asterias) is an echinoderm."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q159","pn":159,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Animal Kingdom","st":"Haploid cells and gametogenesis","q":"In which animal do haploid cells divide mitotically to produce gametes?","o":["Male honeybees","Male grasshoppers","Male earthworms","Male frogs"],"c":0,"s":"The male honeybee is haploid and the female is diploid.\nGamete formation in female honeybees is by meiosis, whereas male honeybees form gametes by mitosis.\nThus haploid cells undergo mitosis in male honeybees."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q171","pn":171,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Animal Kingdom","st":"Phyla characteristics","q":"Select the incorrect statements from the following:\nA. Digestive system in Platyhelminthes is incomplete.\nB. Bilateral symmetry is a characteristic feature of adult Echinoderms.\nC. Pseudocoelom is possessed by Aschelminthes.\nD. Notochord is persistent throughout life in the class Chondrichthyes.\nE. Members of class Reptilia maintain a constant body temperature.\nChoose the answer from the options given below:","o":["B and E only","C and D only","A and C only","B and D only"],"c":0,"s":"A is correct: Platyhelminthes have an incomplete digestive system.\nB is incorrect: bilateral symmetry is a feature of echinoderm larvae. Adult echinoderms show radial symmetry.\nC is correct: Aschelminthes have a pseudocoelom.\nD is correct: the notochord is persistent throughout life in Chondrichthyes.\nE is incorrect: reptiles are cold-blooded, so they cannot maintain a constant body temperature. Warm-blooded birds and mammals can.\nThe incorrect statements are B and E only."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q109","pn":109,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Animal Kingdom","st":"Types of coelom","q":"While trying to find out the characteristic of a newly found animal, a researcher did the histology of adult animal and observed a cavity with presence of mesodermal tissue towards the body wall but no mesodermal tissue was observed towards the alimentary canal. What could be the possible coelome of that animal?","o":["Acoelomate","Pseudocoelomate","Schizocoelomate","Spongocoelomate"],"c":1,"s":"In pseudocoelomates the body cavity is not entirely lined with mesoderm.\nMesodermal tissue is present along the body wall but not towards the gut.\nSchizocoelomates have a coelom that develops from a split in the mesoderm.\nIn acoelomates the coelom is absent.\nSpongocoel is a central cavity found in sponges."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q139","pn":139,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Animal Kingdom","st":"Class Cyclostomata","q":"All living members of the class Cyclostomata are :","o":["Free living","Endoparasite","Symbiotic","Ectoparasite"],"c":3,"s":"All living members of class Cyclostomata are ectoparasites."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q142","pn":142,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Animal Kingdom","st":"Phylum Chordata, vertebrates","q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A) : All vertebrates are chordates but all chordates are not vertebrate.\nReason (R) : The members of subphylum vertebrata possess notochord during the embryonic period, the notochord is replaced by cartilaginous or bony vertebral column in adults.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true"],"c":0,"s":"Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) explains (A).\nMembers of subphylum Vertebrata have a notochord in the embryonic period.\nIn adults the notochord is replaced by a cartilaginous or bony vertebral column.\nThus all vertebrates are chordates but all chordates are not vertebrates."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q166","pn":166,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Animal Kingdom","st":"Echinoderms","q":"Role of the water vascular system in Echinoderms is:\nA. Respiration and Locomotion\nB. Excretion and Locomotion\nC. Capture and transport of food\nD. Digestion and Respiration\nE. Digestion and Excretion\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["A and B Only","A and C Only","B and C Only","B, D and E Only"],"c":1,"s":"The water vascular system of echinoderms helps in locomotion, capture and transport of food, and respiration.\nEchinoderms have no excretory system. Excretion takes place through the general body surface."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q172","pn":172,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Animal Kingdom","st":"Body cavity types","q":"Consider the following statements :\nA. Annelids are true coelomates\nB. Poriferans are pseudocoelomates\nC. Aschelminthes are acoelomates\nD. Platyhelminthes are pseudocoelomates\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["C only","D only","B only","A only"],"c":3,"s":"Option (4) is correct: annelids are true coelomates.\nPoriferans are acoelomates, aschelminthes are pseudocoelomates and platyhelminthes are acoelomates.\nSo B, C and D are wrong."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q176","pn":176,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Animal Kingdom","st":"Phylum characteristics","q":"Match List I with List II :\nA. Pleurobrachia — I. Mollusca\nB. Radule — II. Ctenophora\nC. Stomochord — III. Osteichthyes\nD. Air bladder — IV. Hemichordata","o":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],"c":3,"s":"Option (4) is correct.\nA. Pleurobrachia belongs to phylum Ctenophora.\nB. Radula is a rasping feeding organ of Mollusca.\nC. Stomochord is a small notochord-like structure in the collar region of Hemichordata.\nD. Air bladder is found in Osteichthyes and gives buoyancy."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q182","pn":182,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Animal Kingdom","st":"Fishes: scientific and common names","q":"Match List I with List II:\nA. Pterophyllum — I. Hag fish\nB. Myxine — II. Saw fish\nC. Pristis — III. Angel fish\nD. Exocoetus — IV. Flying fish","o":["A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV"],"c":3,"s":"Pterophyllum is the angel fish.\nMyxine is the hag fish.\nPristis is the saw fish.\nExocoetus is the flying fish.\nSo A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV.\nNote: The printed answer is [4], which is correct; the printed solution says option (2), a misprint."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q186","pn":186,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Animal Kingdom","st":"Chordates vs non-chordates","q":"The following are the statements about non-chordates:\nA. Pharynx is perforated by gill slits.\nB. Notochord is absent.\nC. Central nervous system is dorsal.\nD. Heart is dorsal if present.\nE. Post anal tail is absent.\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","o":["B, D & E only","B, C & D only","A & C only","A, B & D only"],"c":0,"s":"Non-chordate features among these: B (notochord absent), D (heart dorsal if present), E (post-anal tail absent).\nA and C are features of chordates.\nSo option (1)."}
